# =============================================================================
# docker/.env.example - TEMPLATE for the values docker/docker-compose.yml and
#                       docker/docker-compose.tls.yml interpolate.
#
#   cp docker/.env.example docker/.env      # then edit docker/.env
#
# Compose reads `.env` from the directory that CONTAINS the compose file, so it
# must be docker/.env; a .env at the repository root is not read. Exporting the
# same names in the invoking shell works identically and takes precedence, which
# is how a real deployment should supply them - from the orchestrator's secret
# store rather than from a file on disk.
#
# NEVER COMMIT docker/.env. It holds real credentials. .gitignore enforces that
# with `.env*` / `*.env` plus a `!**/.env.example` negation that keeps THIS file,
# and only this file, tracked. Confirm both halves before committing:
#       git check-ignore -v docker/.env           # expect a match: ignored
#       git check-ignore -v docker/.env.example   # expect no match: tracked
# No secret - not a real one, not a convincing sample - is written into a tracked
# file here.
#
# FILE FORMAT. KEY=value, one entry per line, no spaces around `=`, no `export`
# prefix, no quotes, no line continuations; `#` begins a comment. Do not quote a
# value: Compose keeps the quote characters and they become part of the value.
# Semicolons need no escaping - this is not a shell script.
#
# THERE IS NO DATABASE CONTAINER. The compose file declares two services, `api`
# and `frontend`. SQL Server is always EXTERNAL and must already hold the existing
# DotNetNuke schema; the API maps onto that schema and never creates, alters or
# drops any part of it.
#
# WHO CAN READ THESE VALUES. Compose passes each one in as an ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE
# - `ConnectionStrings__Default=${DB_CONNECTION_STRING}`, `Jwt__Secret=${JWT_SECRET}`
# - which is what the preserved container example (AAP 0.9.3) declares. Assume every
# one of these readers:
#   * the container's own metadata, through the Docker socket or API;
#   * the process environment inside the container, for anything that can exec
#     into it or read /proc as the right user;
#   * this file, and any shell history or CI log that carried the values;
#   * a `docker compose config` rendering, which interpolates them.
# To check that a variable is PRESENT without printing it - never grep the value
# itself into a terminal, a ticket or a CI log:
#       docker inspect dnnmigration-api \
#         --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println (index (split . "=") 0)}}{{end}}' \
#         | grep -x Jwt__Secret          # prints only the NAME if it is set
#
# That environment block is the ONLY surface that exposes them: neither value
# appears in either image's history, in its layers, in its configuration, anywhere
# on the API image's filesystem, in the served frontend bundle, or in either
# container's logs - all measured at zero occurrences, including for an issued
# access token.
#
# FOR A DEPLOYMENT THAT CANNOT ACCEPT THAT, THERE IS A SUPPORTED FILE ROUTE THE
# HOST READS:
#   * ONE FILE PER KEY, IN /run/secrets, AND THE FILE NAME IS THE KEY. A double
#     underscore in the file name becomes the section separator, so
#     /run/secrets/ConnectionStrings__Default -> ConnectionStrings:Default and
#     /run/secrets/Jwt__Secret -> Jwt:Secret. Those two are the only keys this API
#     requires from a secret store; the optional LegacyCredentials__DecryptionKey can
#     be delivered the same way. A trailing newline is trimmed.
#   * COMPOSE'S OWN `secrets:` IS THE SMALLEST STEP: it mounts each declared secret at
#     /run/secrets/<target>, so name the target for the key and delete the matching
#     `environment:` line. Docker Swarm and a Kubernetes secret volume produce the
#     same shape; for a Kubernetes PROJECTED volume, set `Secrets__Directory`
#     (Secrets:Directory) to the mount path and the same image reads from there.
#   * A MOUNTED FILE WINS. The source is added after the environment, so a mounted
#     secret outranks an environment variable of the same name - which is the
#     direction a deployment wants, because mounting the file is how the value is kept
#     OUT of the inspectable environment block. Verified by running the API with both
#     values delivered ONLY as files: the container reports healthy, the readiness
#     probe reaches the database, a sign-in returns a usable token, and neither value
#     appears in the environment.
#   * EACH MOUNTED FILE MUST BE READABLE BY UID 1000, the unprivileged account the
#     image creates - mode 0444 is the usual choice and the compose and Kubernetes
#     default. A root-only file is not silently ignored: the host fails to start, with
#     a permission error rather than a message about a missing setting.
#   * A MANAGED SECRET STORE - Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault
#     - is reached either by the orchestrator writing the fetched material into that
#     directory or by an entry-point exporting it into the environment; neither needs
#     a code change. A store read directly through its own configuration provider
#     would, and no such provider is registered here.
# Any of these routes is a DELIBERATE DEVIATION from the preserved compose example
# and belongs in MIGRATION_NOTES.md when taken.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_CONNECTION_STRING                                            *** REQUIRED ***
#
#   Type      SQL Server connection string
#   Maps to   ConnectionStrings__Default -> configuration key ConnectionStrings:Default
#             (`__` is .NET's separator for `:`; a single underscore binds to nothing)
#   Default   NONE. appsettings.json ships an empty value and the Production
#             overlay supplies none
#   Secret    YES - it carries a password. Prefer a secret store over this file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The API validates this at start-up and REFUSES TO START on a value that is blank,
# unparseable, names no server, names no database, names no way to authenticate, or
# still carries a documented placeholder. The refusal names the rule and never
# echoes any part of the value. That strictness is deliberate: the `/health`
# liveness probe excludes the database on purpose, so a container with a broken
# connection string would otherwise report HEALTHY, release the front end behind it,
# and fail every database-backed request and every sign-in.
#
# CERTIFICATE VALIDATION IS THE POINT OF THIS SHAPE. Microsoft.Data.SqlClient has
# defaulted to `Encrypt=True` since 4.0, so the driver always negotiates TLS; what
# `TrustServerCertificate=True` adds is the silent DISABLING of the validation that
# makes the encryption worth having. The examples below therefore omit it, and
# expect a certificate the client already trusts (install the SQL Server
# certificate's issuer into the container's trust store, or issue the server a
# certificate from an authority already in it).
#
# `host.docker.internal` is the container's route back to the machine running
# Docker; the compose file maps it to host-gateway so the name also resolves on
# Linux. Never write `localhost` here - inside a container that is the container.
#
# DO NOT ADD `MultipleActiveResultSets=True`. A common addition to a legacy
# DotNetNuke connection string, and the damage is silent: Microsoft.Data.SqlClient
# cannot take a transaction savepoint on a MARS connection, so EF Core proceeds
# without one and logs `SavepointsDisabledBecauseOfMARS` and nothing else. This API
# has multi-step SERIALIZABLE transactions that depend on savepoints - tenant removal
# deletes a tenant's modules before the tenant itself, because that foreign key is
# the one in this schema with no cascade - and without them a failure part-way
# through cannot be unwound to a known point. The keyword buys this API nothing: EF
# Core reads each query to completion and every I/O path is awaited.
#
# Shapes to copy into the active line below. Replace <sql-login> and <sql-password>
# with a real credential, which belongs in a secret store. `Server=` takes
# `host.docker.internal` for SQL Server on the Docker host, a resolvable name for a
# remote server, or the service name for another container on the same network:
#     DB_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=host.docker.internal,1433;Database=DotNetNuke;User Id=<sql-login>;Password=<sql-password>;Encrypt=True
#
#   LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY - a self-signed server certificate, validation disabled.
#   Never deploy this shape:
#     DB_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=host.docker.internal,1433;Database=DotNetNuke;User Id=<sql-login>;Password=<sql-password>;TrustServerCertificate=True;Encrypt=True
#
# Windows account authentication is NOT USABLE from this container: the legacy
# `Data Source` form relied on a per-account Express instance, a database file
# attached at run time and Windows authentication, none of which exist in the Linux
# Alpine image the API runs in. Use SQL authentication, or an identity mechanism
# supported on Linux.
#
# The value below is EMPTY on purpose, so an unedited copy fails loudly at start-up
# rather than quietly at the first query.
DB_CONNECTION_STRING=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWT_SECRET                                                      *** REQUIRED ***
#
#   Type      symmetric signing key, at least 32 UTF-8 bytes
#   Maps to   Jwt__Secret -> configuration key Jwt:Secret
#   Default   NONE. appsettings.json ships an empty value and no overlay supplies one
#   Secret    YES - it signs and verifies every access token
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Start-up enforces all three of these before the API serves traffic:
#   * at least 32 UTF-8 BYTES - 256 bits, the output width of the SHA-256 that
#     HMAC-SHA256 is built on, so a shorter key adds no strength the algorithm can
#     use;
#   * at least 8 distinct characters, which rejects a long run of one character that
#     satisfies the length rule with almost no entropy;
#   * no well-known placeholder fragment, matched case-insensitively.
#
# The value below trips that third rule ON PURPOSE: a deployment that forgets to
# replace it fails at start-up rather than signing real tokens with a key published
# in this repository.
#
# Generate a fresh key per environment from a cryptographic random source and hold
# it in a managed secret store rather than in this file:
#       openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_GENERATE_A_RANDOM_KEY_OF_AT_LEAST_32_BYTES


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional overrides
#
# Compose supplies a fallback for each of these, so they ship COMMENTED OUT: an
# entry left accidentally blank would shadow a perfectly good default. Uncomment
# only what a deployment genuinely has to change, and give it a value when you do.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# JWT_ISSUER -> Jwt__Issuer -> Jwt:Issuer. Written to the `iss` claim of every token
# issued and checked on every token presented, so a blank value disables that check
# rather than passing it. Compose fallback: DnnMigration.
#JWT_ISSUER=DnnMigration

# JWT_AUDIENCE -> Jwt__Audience -> Jwt:Audience. Written to `aud` and checked the
# same way. Compose fallback: DnnMigration. Issuer and audience hold the same string
# deliberately: this API is a backend-for-frontend with exactly one client, so it is
# the only audience for the tokens it issues. A deployment fronting further clients
# would separate the two.
#JWT_AUDIENCE=DnnMigration

# JWT_EXPIRATION_MINUTES -> Jwt__ExpirationMinutes -> Jwt:ExpirationMinutes.
# Access-token lifetime in minutes; a positive whole number. Compose fallback: 60,
# which preserves the legacy authentication ticket timeout exactly. Lowering it
# narrows the window in which a leaked token stays usable, at the cost of more
# traffic on the refresh endpoint.
#JWT_EXPIRATION_MINUTES=60

# REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_PROVIDER -> RefreshTokenStore__Provider ->
# RefreshTokenStore:Provider. Declares WHICH refresh-token store this deployment
# runs. The accepted names are a closed set - an unrecognised name stops the
# container rather than falling back to the default. Compose fallback: InProcess.
#
#   InProcess   the process-local store this image ships, and the default.
#               `InMemory` is accepted as an alias of it.
#   SqlServer   the shared, durable store this repository also ships. Refresh
#               families then survive a restart and every replica observes the same
#               revocations. It needs REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_CONNECTION, and that
#               catalogue MUST NOT be the DotNetNuke database - see that variable.
#   External    a store the DEPLOYMENT registers itself. It enables nothing; it
#               ASSERTS that an IRefreshTokenStore other than this repository's has
#               been registered after AddInfrastructure (the last registration of a
#               service wins), and the API verifies the assertion while it starts.
#               Against THIS image it correctly stops the container, because no
#               replacement is registered in it.
#
# LEFT AT InProcess, REFRESH STATE IS PROCESS-LOCAL: it lives in the API process, so
# it is neither shared between replicas nor carried across a restart, which is why
# the compose file publishes a single api service. A revocation that cannot be
# confirmed is reported as UNCONFIRMED rather than as a completed sign-out, so no
# session is ever silently left live - the client keeps its credential and retries.
# The `refresh-token-store` health probe reports the mode, whether it is
# replica-safe and whether it survives a restart. See the operating notes in
# README.md.
#REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_PROVIDER=InProcess

# REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_SINGLE_INSTANCE ->
# RefreshTokenStore__AcknowledgeSingleInstance ->
# RefreshTokenStore:AcknowledgeSingleInstance. Compose fallback: true, because the
# compose file starts exactly one api service.
#
# IN PRODUCTION THE API REFUSES TO START on a session store that is neither shared
# between replicas nor carried across a restart unless the deployment states that it
# runs a single instance. That is this variable. It CHANGES NO BEHAVIOUR and grants no
# capability; its only function is to make the claim explicit, because scaling out on
# the process-local store needs no code or configuration change and produces no
# warning, while a sign-out against one replica leaves the session exchangeable on
# every other and a restart forgets every family it issued - both of which present as
# intermittent session behaviour that no log explains.
#
# Set it ONLY where a single instance is genuinely enforced by the topology. If you
# run more than one, do not set it: set REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_PROVIDER=SqlServer with a
# session catalogue instead, which satisfies the requirement on its own merits.
# Non-production environments are unaffected either way, deliberately - an
# acknowledgement demanded everywhere gets set reflexively and stops meaning
# anything.
#REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_SINGLE_INSTANCE=true

# REFRESH_TOKEN_MAX_TRACKED -> RefreshTokenStore__MaximumTrackedTokens ->
# RefreshTokenStore:MaximumTrackedTokens. How many refresh-token generations the
# in-process store tracks at once; must be between 1000 and 1000000, and the API
# refuses to start outside that range. Compose fallback: 100000, which is roughly
# ten megabytes of tracked state and far above any ordinary working set - one caller
# refreshing every half hour for a full thirty-day family lifetime accounts for
# about 1,400 generations. At the ceiling the OLDEST refresh families are retired
# first, so their holders sign in again while every request continues to be served,
# and the `refresh-token-store` health probe reports Degraded with the usage. Raise
# it only if that probe says you need to; past a million the honest answer is a
# shared store rather than a larger per-replica dictionary.
#REFRESH_TOKEN_MAX_TRACKED=100000

# REFRESH_TOKEN_GRACE_SECONDS -> RefreshTokenStore__ConcurrentUseGraceSeconds ->
# RefreshTokenStore:ConcurrentUseGraceSeconds. The window, in seconds, in which a
# refresh token already spent may be presented AGAIN by the same client without
# being treated as theft; 0 to 60, Compose fallback 5. It exists because an
# interrupted exchange - a dropped response, a retried request - would otherwise
# revoke the caller's whole token family. Zero is the strictest setting and treats
# every reuse as a replay; a long window weakens replay detection, which is why 60
# is the ceiling.
#REFRESH_TOKEN_GRACE_SECONDS=5

# REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_CONNECTION -> RefreshTokenStore__ConnectionString. The
# catalogue holding the SHARED refresh-token store's single table, read only when
# REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_PROVIDER selects SqlServer.
#
# THE CATALOGUE MUST NOT BE THE DOTNETNUKE DATABASE, and the API enforces it at
# start-up rather than trusting the operator. Three rules are applied, and all three
# refuse the host outright: the connection string must name its catalogue explicitly
# through `Database=` or `Initial Catalog=`; that catalogue must not be one of the
# server's own administrative databases (master, model, msdb, tempdb); and its NAME
# must differ from the catalogue named by DB_CONNECTION_STRING. The comparison is by
# catalogue name rather than by whole connection string on purpose - a host is
# spellable as localhost, 127.0.0.1, (local), a machine name or a named instance, so
# a rule that compared hosts could be bypassed by writing one differently.
#
# PROVISION THE TABLE BEFORE STARTING THE API. It is created by a deployment step,
# not by the running application, which neither creates nor alters it: create the
# catalogue and then run docker/sql/refresh-token-store.sql against it. The API
# probes for the table on first use and reports session operations as unavailable -
# and says so on its health endpoint - while it is absent. A configuration that sets
# RefreshTokenStore__CreateTableIfMissing is REFUSED at start-up with a message
# naming that script, because letting the API create its own table is schema
# authorship at runtime by the process that serves requests.
#
# USE A PRINCIPAL THAT CANNOT DO MORE THAN THE STORE NEEDS. Do not reuse `sa`, and
# do not reuse the principal that ran the provisioning script. The API needs exactly
# four rights on exactly one table:
#
#   CREATE USER [dnnmigration_api] FOR LOGIN [dnnmigration_api];
#   GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
#     ON [dbo].[DnnMigrationRefreshTokens] TO [dnnmigration_api];
#
# ENCRYPT THE CONNECTION AND VALIDATE THE CERTIFICATE. `Encrypt=True` is required:
# refresh-token digests and the session's account and tenant identifiers cross this
# connection. Leave `TrustServerCertificate` OUT so the server's certificate is
# verified against a trust chain - a trusted certificate is what makes the
# encryption resistant to interception rather than merely present. It belongs ONLY
# on a developer machine talking to a local container with a self-signed
# certificate.
#
#   REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_PROVIDER=SqlServer
#   REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_CONNECTION=Server=sql,1433;Database=DnnMigrationSessions;User Id=dnnmigration_api;Password=<password>;Encrypt=True
#
#   # local development ONLY - accepts any certificate the server presents
#   REFRESH_TOKEN_STORE_CONNECTION=Server=localhost,1433;Database=DnnMigrationSessions;User Id=dnnmigration_api;Password=<password>;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True

# FRONTEND_ORIGIN -> Cors__AllowedOrigins__0 -> Cors:AllowedOrigins[0]. The one
# exact browser origin allowed to call the API cross-origin; the trailing `__0` is
# .NET's spelling for the first element of an array setting. Give a scheme, host and
# port with no trailing slash and no path. Compose fallback: http://localhost:4200,
# the port the base compose file publishes the SPA on. A wildcard is never
# acceptable here: the API has no legitimate browser caller it cannot name, and the
# policy deliberately never enables ambient browser credentials.
#
# READ BY THE BASE TOPOLOGY ONLY. The containerised SPA is same-origin through the
# proxy and needs no cross-origin permission at all, so this entry exists for a
# workstation running the SPA's own dev server against the container. The TLS
# overlay does NOT read it: it derives the permitted origin from DNN_PUBLIC_HOST
# instead, because two variables that must agree are two variables that can
# disagree. One public identity, four consumers.
#
# A host name reserved for documentation is REFUSED here at start-up: an origin on
# example.com, example.net, example.org or the .example top-level domain can never
# be sent by a browser, so a policy configured with one would admit nothing while
# looking configured. Names a private deployment legitimately uses - .test,
# .invalid, .localhost, localhost, an address literal - are accepted.
#FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:4200

# TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESS -> Proxy__KnownProxies__0 -> Proxy:KnownProxies[0]. The ONE
# reverse-proxy address permitted to report the original client's address and scheme
# through X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto. Compose fallback: 172.28.0.10, the
# fixed compose address of the frontend (nginx) container.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS. The API's credential rate limiter partitions on the caller's
# address. With no trusted proxy the framework's default trust - loopback only -
# applies, so inside a container the forwarded headers are ignored and the address
# the limiter observes is the reverse proxy's for every browser in the deployment.
# All callers then share ONE credential budget and any single client can exhaust
# everybody else's.
#
# GIVE ONE ADDRESS, NOT A RANGE. A forwarded address is supplied by whoever made the
# request, so trusting a hop you do not operate lets a caller name its own address
# and therefore choose its own rate-limit partition. Trusting the compose subnet is
# NOT equivalent and is not safe here: the api service publishes 8080 on the host,
# and a request arriving through that published port is source-translated to the
# network gateway, which is itself a subnet address - so a direct caller on the host
# would be trusted.
#
# Override it only if you have changed that address or you front the API with a
# different proxy. A deployment that terminates TLS and rate-limits AT the proxy can
# set this to an address nothing uses, in which case the API's own limiter degrades
# to one shared budget - more restrictive, and acceptable only when something else
# is doing the per-caller accounting.
#
# Only the forwarded ADDRESS and SCHEME are honoured. The forwarded HOST is not:
# this API resolves the tenant from the host name, so accepting a forwarded host
# would let a caller behind a trusted proxy choose which tenant it is served.
#TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESS=172.28.0.10

# LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED / _ENABLED_UNTIL_UTC / _DECRYPTION_KEY ->
# LegacyCredentials__Enabled / __EnabledUntilUtc / __DecryptionKey. Compose default:
# Enabled=false with empty companions, the normal post-migration state. THE
# DECRYPTION KEY IS A SECRET, absolutely.
#
# Existing DotNetNuke accounts may still hold a credential in the membership
# provider's legacy clear, SHA-1 or machine-key-encrypted format. The API can verify
# such a value ONCE and replace it immediately with BCrypt, but the compatibility
# path is OFF by default and expires at one absolute UTC instant. Enable all three
# together:
#   LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED=true
#   LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED_UNTIL_UTC=YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
#   LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_DECRYPTION_KEY=<READ_FROM_SECRET_STORE>
#
# The key is the legacy machine-key decryption value, supplied at deployment time
# from a managed secret store. NEVER paste it into this tracked template, into
# appsettings.json, into a committed compose override, into a log, or into a support
# ticket. Start-up validates its length, its hexadecimal alphabet and its Triple-DES
# strength, and never includes the value in an error.
#
# The deadline is ABSOLUTE rather than relative to process start, so restarting the
# container cannot renew the window, and start-up refuses an enabled window with no
# deadline or a non-UTC one. Once it passes, accounts that have not signed in use
# the administrator-reset path. Leaving all three unset is the normal configuration:
# compose passes Enabled=false with empty companions, start-up accepts that disabled
# shape, and the verifier fails closed. MIGRATION_NOTES.md Section 4.1 is the full
# account.
#LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED=false
#LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED_UNTIL_UTC=YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
#LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_DECRYPTION_KEY=READ_FROM_SECRET_STORE


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TLS overlay only
#
# Both are meaningless without docker/docker-compose.tls.yml, so they ship
# commented out; the overlay declares NO fallback for either, so compose refuses to
# create a container until both are set. Supply them on the command line and edit no
# tracked file:
#
#   DNN_PUBLIC_HOST=admin.acme.test \
#   TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY=/etc/letsencrypt/live/admin.acme.test \
#   docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml \
#                  -f docker/docker-compose.tls.yml \
#                  --env-file docker/.env up -d
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# DNN_PUBLIC_HOST - THE ONE PUBLIC IDENTITY OF A DEPLOYMENT. Read only by the TLS
# overlay, which passes it to FOUR consumers that must agree with one another:
#   1. the reverse proxy's two `server_name` directives, rendered into
#      docker/nginx.tls.conf.template by the nginx image's own entrypoint - which is
#      what decides whose port-80 requests are redirected to HTTPS;
#   2. the API's `AllowedHosts`, as `${DNN_PUBLIC_HOST};localhost;127.0.0.1` - the
#      loopback entries stay so the container health probe keeps working;
#   3. the API's permitted browser origin, as `https://${DNN_PUBLIC_HOST}`;
#   4. the certificate TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY must contain, which has to be valid
#      for this name.
#
# GIVE A BARE HOST NAME: no scheme, no port, no path, no trailing dot - for example
# `admin.acme.test`. Add a port only if the deployment publishes 443 somewhere other
# than 443, in which case the port belongs in the browser origin rather than here.
#
# THE VALUE BELOW IS DELIBERATELY ONE THE API REFUSES TO START ON. `.example` is
# reserved for documentation by RFC 6761, and the API rejects it - along with
# example.com, example.net, example.org and unreplaced markers such as `changeme` -
# precisely so that copying this illustration fails closed at start-up instead of
# serving traffic under a name nothing else agrees on. Reserved TEST domains are NOT
# refused: `.test`, `.invalid` and `.localhost` are what a private deployment or an
# internal certificate authority legitimately uses.
#DNN_PUBLIC_HOST=admin.acme.example

# TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY - THE ONLY CERTIFICATE CONTRACT IN THIS REPOSITORY. Read
# only by the TLS overlay, which mounts it read-only into the front-end container;
# the TLS server block rendered from docker/nginx.tls.conf.template reads
# fullchain.pem and privkey.pem from inside it.
#
# Point it at a host directory holding exactly two files:
#     fullchain.pem   the server certificate and its issuing chain
#     privkey.pem     the matching private key
# Both are mounted read-only at /etc/nginx/tls/certificates - a directory the proxy's
# own `*.conf` include glob cannot match, so a mounted certificate can never be
# parsed as configuration. Keep the private key out of this repository and out of any
# image: it is mounted at run time and nothing copies it.
#
# THE CERTIFICATE MUST BE VALID FOR DNN_PUBLIC_HOST. That is the same name the
# proxy's server_name and the API's host filter are rendered from, so a mismatch is a
# certificate problem only - never a configuration disagreement between the three.
#
# *** THE KEY PAIR MUST BE READABLE BY UID 101, AND A DEFAULT-PERMISSIONED KEY IS
# *** NOT. SEC-12.
#
# The proxy container runs UNPRIVILEGED, as uid 101 - the image's own nginx account -
# so the private key is opened by uid 101, and a root-owned mode 0600 key (what
# certbot and openssl produce by default) cannot be opened at all. nginx refuses to
# start with `[emerg] cannot load certificate key ... BIO_new_file() failed (SSL: ...
# Permission denied ...)`. Grant read access to uid 101 and no more than that:
#
#     chown 101:101 <dir>/fullchain.pem <dir>/privkey.pem
#     chmod 640     <dir>/privkey.pem      # 644 on fullchain.pem is fine; it is public
#
# Or, where the host manages certificates centrally and the files must keep their
# owner, add uid 101 to a group the key already grants read to. Do NOT make the key
# world-readable to get past this: 0644 on a private key is a far larger exposure
# than the permission error it silences. Both outcomes were measured against this
# image.
#
# A LET'S ENCRYPT DIRECTORY NEEDS ONE EXTRA STEP: the live path holds symlinks into
# ../../archive, so the ownership has to be applied to the archive files the links
# resolve to, and re-applied after each renewal - a renewal hook is the usual place
# for it.
#TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY=/etc/letsencrypt/live/admin.acme.example


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Names nothing reads
#
# Recorded because a deployment upgrading from an earlier copy of this template may
# have these lines in a live .env file, where they do nothing.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH and TLS_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH - no compose file, Dockerfile or
#   nginx configuration reads either name. The certificate contract is the DIRECTORY
#   documented above as TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY.
# TLS_ALLOWED_HOSTS - the TLS overlay composes the API's host filter from
#   DNN_PUBLIC_HOST instead, as `${DNN_PUBLIC_HOST};localhost;127.0.0.1`, keeping the
#   two loopback entries the container health probe needs: it addresses the API on
#   the loopback address, so dropping them would make the api service permanently
#   unhealthy and the front end would never start.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not variables - settings that are fixed in the compose files
#
# Listed so their absence from this template is not read as an omission. Each is
# stated as the compose files currently declare it; change it there, not here.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT is hard-coded to Production on the api service in
#   docker-compose.yml and is not interpolated.
# The Kestrel listen address is baked into the image as ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://+:8080
#   by docker/api.Dockerfile. docker-compose.yml deliberately does not restate it.
# Published ports are fixed by the compose files: the API on 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 -
#   LOOPBACK ONLY, so its cleartext listener is not reachable off this host - and the
#   SPA on 4200:80. The TLS overlay withdraws the API's published port entirely and
#   publishes 80 and 443 for the front end.
# Https__RedirectEnabled is set explicitly in BOTH compose files: false in
#   docker-compose.yml, because appsettings.Production.json enables redirection and
#   every non-health path would otherwise be answered with a redirect this cleartext
#   topology cannot satisfy; true in docker-compose.tls.yml, together with
#   Https__RedirectPort=443.
# AllowedHosts is set only by the TLS overlay, from DNN_PUBLIC_HOST above. The base
#   topology inherits `*` from appsettings.json.
# Proxy__KnownProxies__0 IS interpolated, from TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESS above, and it
#   defaults to the front end's fixed compose address - so the base topology trusts
#   exactly one hop rather than nothing. Proxy__KnownNetworks is not set anywhere:
#   trusting a range would also trust the gateway that host traffic to the published
#   API port is translated to.
# The SPA's API base address is not a run-time value at all. It is baked into the
#   Angular bundle at build time as the RELATIVE path /api/v1, which nginx proxies to
#   the api service, so the browser always calls the origin that served it.
# Database credentials arrive only inside DB_CONNECTION_STRING. There are
#   deliberately no separate account, password or server variables, and no database
#   administrator password, because this topology starts no database container.
# Nothing here configures a cache, a message broker, a mail server or a telemetry
#   endpoint; the API uses none of them.
# No legacy DotNetNuke setting appears here. The provider attributes that qualified
#   legacy object names have no counterpart - table and schema names are fixed in the
#   Infrastructure layer's entity configurations - and the machine-level keys the
#   legacy configuration committed are covered by JWT_SECRET and, for the bounded
#   migration window only, LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_DECRYPTION_KEY.
